 operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7bbd11558f95d5c63611e864d4e4f9e2d18f6764e1cd0e12fd343d300266d8e9"} {"question" "q-131999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-31946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-31946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-31946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7bbd11558f95d5c63611e864d4e4f9e2d18f6764e1cd0e12fd343d300266d8e9"} {"question" "q-31946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-31946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-31946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7bbd11558f95d5c63611e864d4e4f9e2d18f6764e1cd0e12fd343d300266d8e9"} {"question" "q-131386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-131386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $V$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7bbd11558f95d5c63611e864d4e4f9e2d18f6764e1cd0e12fd343d300266d8e9"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7bbd11558f95d5c63611e864d4e4f9e2d18f6764e1cd0e12fd343d300266d8e9"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ffc8b4a03dfe593118f9cee0c0a35b5918c06744f0afe98fb5c5535e368a477"} {"question" "e-4725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4725.json" "file_sha256" "b25598b27300e7dcc6343e94b9aa8745f6565b2e8c2088d1ec6a996b8074b06d"} {"question" "e-4725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4725.json" "file_sha256" "b25598b27300e7dcc6343e94b9aa8745f6565b2e8c2088d1ec6a996b8074b06d"} {"question" "e-4724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4724.json" "file_sha256" "d2b4bfcdd3824180497413575fc8ed97f84884adca0406f94bcaf9b92947c812"} {"question" "e-4724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/247c0a29294e61735b969d769d8ba3794c0b5e816c3968a8db087740eeb925d8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4724.json" "file_sha256" "d2b4bfcdd3824180497413575fc8ed97f84884adca0406f94bcaf9b92947c812"} {"question" "e-4723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4723.json" "file_sha256" "a37e2dec97ef60ee61141d71d52c168514b0a0f0360998423e0275df0a77972a"} {"question" "e-4723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/694b10f1d1c18f25e079e9e7cc75e9cc457ef01d0c229f006308fd667108459b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4723.json" "file_sha256" "a37e2dec97ef60ee61141d71d52c168514b0a0f0360998423e0275df0a77972a"} {"question" "q-42988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "6156c56f9057cb5777bbc3183f8d5e1ae7637928ed1daea2976a0e4c46546f65" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "After R2:=R2-2R1, the augmented second row is [0,5,-2,1]. Dividing it by 5 gives [0,1,-2/5,1/5], while the first row remains [1,0,1,0]. The left block is now the identity, so the inverse is the right block [[1,0],[-2/5,1/5]], exactly original option 1 and the stored answer. This is a source-backed derived judgment corroborated by the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grade." "bindings" {"problem" "057dc7bb7b931a23afc32f8e68a4e082a74c1944b0f27b0f4425410f37c3e491" "worked_solution" "de06aaa7d947bce9d538e2cd6dbec25a5ca8e0cbfea9b241a1c9d9c2e5a5f15d" "original_field" "c27ae3d23bb2055399a8d24caf07ebe4179be169655333eea7b098bdb65fba1b" "reviewed_field" "54777fd866eaa2958726c09d56e3a3008a17872bb760d177c2d36d30412f0a7d" "raw_widget" "344761a2885d1c92c070e40a214181c2ad16ca16f303ddc00cb821012bab05a5"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-42988-before.png" "sha256" "23ce1cfd649860dc0bd26aecd5eca752e2f3ce1e99b8ceaafbe356abb4711ffd"} {"path" "history-q-42988.png" "sha256" "32026c580de9ff8724d2c543c18c92b7f78cedc7654b6becba78e6a9156a3097"} {"path" "q-42988-after.json" "sha256" "ae0fafa37d5d3b91079164d50a63beb3db7099c1ba40cd77067e4085ab6bf92a"}]} {"question" "q-42988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "6156c56f9057cb5777bbc3183f8d5e1ae7637928ed1daea2976a0e4c46546f65" "rationale" "After R2:=R2-2R1, the augmented second row is [0,5,-2,1]. Dividing it by 5 gives [0,1,-2/5,1/5], while the first row remains [1,0,1,0]. The left block is now the identity, so the inverse is the right block [[1,0],[-2/5,1/5]], exactly original option 1 and the stored answer. This is a source-backed derived judgment corroborated by the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grade."}] "usage" {"e-4723" [] "q-42988" [] "e-4725" [] "e-4724" []} "basis" 1555 "retractions" [[17592186115760 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & a & a \\\\ 0 & 1 & a & a\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186115760 :question/answer-fields 17592186113931] [17592186114997 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2:= R_2+(-1)R_1$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114997 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\ne I\\cdot \\\\\nB &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."] [17592186114996 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & a & a \\\\ 0 & 1 & a & a\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114996 :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114995 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}___________ \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114995 :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-3)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1:= \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1, \\qquad R_1:= R_1+(-3)R_2\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113932 17592186113933 17592186113934 17592186113935 17592186113936] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113931 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113931 :answer-field/id] [17592186113931 :answer-field/key] [17592186113931 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
